1	//! Regression coverage for the keygen file-permission contract on Unix.
2	//!
3	//! What is protected here (see README "文件权限"):
4	//!
5	//! * Under umask 0022 / 0000 / 0777 a key is generated successfully only as
6	//!   exactly 32 raw bytes with final mode exactly 0600 -- owner read+write,
7	//!   no group/other bits, no extra bits; 0400/0200/0000 are not success.
8	//! * The file is never group/other-accessible, not even for an instant: the
9	//!   mode observed right after creation already carries no group/other bits.
10	//! * Permissions are both set to and *confirmed* as exactly 0600 before any
11	//!   key byte is written. This cannot be observed from the final file, so a
12	//!   small LD_PRELOAD shim (tests/support/permfail) traces the program's
13	//!   open/fchmod/fstat/write calls from inside the process.
14	//! * If the filesystem rejects the required mode (fchmod fails) or the mode
15	//!   cannot be confirmed (fstat fails or reports something other than 0600),
16	//!   keygen exits 1, reports the permission problem on stderr, prints no
17	//!   success message, writes no key bytes, and removes the partial file --
18	//!   without touching pre-existing files, directories, symlinks, or the
19	//!   parent directory. If the system also refuses to remove this run's
20	//!   file, stderr keeps the permission problem, reports the failed cleanup,
21	//!   and warns that this run's file may still be present at the target.
22	//! * Existing CLI behaviour (--version, usage/exit code 2) is unchanged.
